## What to do

1. Use the **reaperc2-operator** skill to sign in to ReaperC2 at $REAPER_URL,
   confirm or create the engagement workspace named "{{.Engagement}}" for
   client "{{.Client}}" (and no other), set its Notes & ATT&CK matrix version
   to v19, and **take notes in ReaperC2 as you work** — engagement notes, tactic
   notes, and technique tags. Those tags are what the ATT&CK Navigator layer
   export is built from.
2. Use the **red-team-operator** skill to plan phases against the objectives above,
   mapping every step to a MITRE ATT&CK v19 tactic/technique. Hand off exact
   commands/beacon actions to **reaperc2-operator** to queue. After each
   meaningful action, write the corresponding Notes & ATT&CK tags in ReaperC2.
3. When a confirmed vulnerability needs a working proof-of-concept, use the
   **exploit-development** skill. It will attach a mandatory educational/
   authorized-use notice to anything it produces — keep that notice intact.
4. For every positive finding (a technique that succeeded — a detection/control
   gap), use the **purple-team-atomic-tests** skill **as soon as the technique
   succeeds** (do not wait until close). Produce real Atomic Red Team files under
   `atomics/<id>/` (`<id>.yaml` and `<id>.md` with an Engagement evidence section
   citing the engagement command **and** its ReaperC2 command output — do not
   invent stdout). A Validation sentence in a report is not a substitute for
   those files.
5. At the end of the engagement (objectives met, time-boxed close, blocked later
   objective, or on request), use the **harness-report** skill to draft the client
   report against `v3-ghostwriter-executive-document.docx`, pulling from
   everything gathered in steps 1-4, including the Navigator layer exported from
   the notes you took.

**Skill-4 gate (do not skip).** Do not start **harness-report** while any
